## ABSTRACT
Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  have  attracted  widespread  interest  due  to  their  unique  and  unprecedented  advantages  in microstructures and properties. Besides, surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) technology has also rapidly developed into a powerful fingerprint spectroscopic technique that can provide rapid, non-invasive, non-destructive, and ultra-sensitive detection, even  down  to  single  molecular  level.  Consequently,  a  considerable  amount  of  researchers  combined  MOFs  with  the  SERS technique to further improve the sensing performance and broaden the applications of SERS substrates. Herein, representative synthesis  strategies  of  MOFs  to  fabricate  SERS-active  substrates  are  summarized  and  their  applications  in  ultra-sensitive biomedical trace detection are also reviewed. Besides, relative barriers, advantages, disadvantages, future trends, and prospects are particularly discussed to give guidance to relevant researchers.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic  framework  (MOF),  surface-enhanced  Raman  scattering  (SERS),  synthesis  strategy,  biomedical  trace detection
## Introduction
Surface-enhanced  Raman  scattering  (SERS)  technology  has rapidly  developed  into  a  powerful  fingerprint  spectroscopic technique  that  can  provide  rapid,  non-invasive,  non-destructive, and ultra-sensitive detection, even down to single molecular level [1–4]. In brevity, when the incident light of a specific frequency irradiates  the  sample  surface,  part  of  the  energy  of  the  incident photons  is  transferred  with  the  molecules,  resulting  in  the frequency of the scattered light no longer being the same as that of the  incident  light  [5].  Thus,  the  frequency  variation  of  Raman scattering  contains  unique  molecular  vibration  information  and the  scattering  intensity  is  directly  proportional  to  the  molecular concentration,  so  that  it  can  be  used  for  qualitative  and quantitative detection and analysis [6]. The principal drawback is a small  cross-section  of  conventional  Raman  scattering  [7, 8].  In SERS,  Raman  scattering  is  enhanced  due  to  near-field  effects, appearing by binding the target molecules to noble metals [7, 8]. Hitherto  two  mainstream  kinds  of  SERS-active  substrates  are plasmonic  materials  (Au,  Ag,  Cu,  and  Al)  with  electromagnetic enhancement  mechanism  (EM)  and  the  other  advanced nanomaterials with chemical enhancement mechanism (CM) [9, 10]. Nowadays, SERS has broad application prospects in materials characterization,  analytical  science,  and  biomedicine  [11–13].
Particularly, exploring emerging synthesis strategy to attain SERSactive substrates is vital for the further development of its practical applications.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous materials  that  combine  organic  and  inorganic  elements  [14]. Usually,  MOFs  are  built  from  organic  linkers  and  metal ions/clusters  featured  with  orderly  gird  frameworks.  As  an attractive  class  of  porous  coordination  polymers,  MOFs  have various unique structures and properties, such as active metal sites, large surface area, excellent adsorption, and chemical stability [15]. Thus, the broad applications in adsorption, sensing, gas storage, gas  separation,  biomedical  trace  detection,  drug  delivery,  and catalysis  have  drawn  great  attention  from  researchers  [16–18]. Over  the  past  few  decades,  MOFs  for  chemical  sensing  have attracted widespread interest and achieved remarkable development  [19–25].  Recent  works  reported  several  singlecomponent MOFs and MOF combinations with other materials (e.g., noble metal materials) which can function as excellent SERSactive  substrates  for  high  sensitivity  detection  [23, 24, 26–28]. Therefore, the emerging synthesis strategies of MOFs to fabricate SERS-active  substrates  imply  a  novel  and  bright  future  for designing future SERS-active materials. Compared with traditional SERS-active materials, owing to being frequently applied as solidphase  micro-extraction  materials  [29],  MOFs  also  have  great potential  to  further  improve  chemical  enhancement  effects  and sensing performance of SERS through molecular enrichment and selective absorption. These properties are quite suitable for dealing with  complex  background  interference  and  situations  in biomedical trace detection like human exhalation, body fluids and tissues.  Particularly,  because  of  the  easy  customization  and modification of MOFs, it is relatively feasible and convenient to design  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  for  the  detection  of specific  biomarkers  [30].  The  stability  of  MOFs  can  also  keep traditional SERS-active metal nanoparticles (NPs) from oxidation and improve biocompatibility for SERS substrates [31, 32]. Herein, we reviewed the recent MOF-based SERS-active substrates and the relative details concerning their fabrication and their biomedical application. The potential application and developing breakthrough of this kind of novel material have been gradually revealed.
## 2    Strategy  of  synthesizing  a  variety  of  MOFbased SERS-active substrates
Three primary strategies have been developed to fabricate MOFbased  SERS-active  substrates,  including  (1)  single-component MOF  substrates,  (2)  core–shell  MOF  substrates,  (3)  SERS-active substrates  based  on  plasmonic  nanoparticles  embedded  or attached porous MOF, and (4) other synthetic strategies. To give a clear vision and specify the merits of each strategy, more details are summarized.
## 2.1    Single-component MOF substrates
Although  most  of  the  MOF  materials  undecorated  with  metal colloids or enhancing agents are generally SERS-inactive, a few of these  solely  single-component  MOFs  still  exhibit  excellent  SERS effects. Normally, single-component MOF substrates are rationally designed for specific analyte molecules through manipulation of their metal ions and organic linkers, so that the micro-pore size, the  energy  band  matching,  and  charge  transfer  between  MOF substrates  and  analyte  molecules  are  adjusted.  Finally,  efficient resonance  combinations  can  be  achieved,  such  as  molecules resonance,  interband  transition  resonance,  light-induced  charge transfer transitions, and ground state charge transfer, resulting in different SERS activities [33].
Zhao  et  al.  demonstrated  that  the  tailorability  of  MOFs achieved through careful modulation of the metal center, organic linker,  and  framework  topology  to  change  the  pore-structure, surface  characteristics,  and  energy  band  can  transform  MOF materials from SERS-inactive to SERS-active substrates, which is comparable  to  the  enhancement  factors  (EFs)  of  noble  metals without “hot  spots” and  recently  reported  semiconductors  [34]. As well, Lisn et al. first interpreted the SERS effect of solely singlecomponent MOFs (MIL-100 and MIL-101) based on the spectral assignment  and  density  functional  theory  (DFT)  calculations  in Figs. 1(a) and 1(b) [29]. In addition, due to the aromatic ligandengineered  MOF,  benzene  compounds  like  aromatic  volatile organic compounds (VOCs) were preferred to be adsorbed into the  micro-pores  of  MOF  via  strong  π–π  or  coordination interactions.  Considering  the  highest  occupied  molecular  orbital (HOMO)  levels  and  the  lowest  unoccupied  molecular  orbital (LUMO) levels of the VOCs to be detected as well as the valence band  (VB)  and  conduction  band  (CB)  of  the  single-component MOFs,  Fu  et  al.  demonstrated  that  MIL-100(Fe),  MIL-125(Ti), ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ ,  and  MIL-101(Cr)  could  serve  as  SERS-active substrates  for  diverse  VOCs  capture  and  recognition,  and  the Raman enhancement mechanism is shown in Fig. 1(c) [26].
Notably,  ultra-thin  2D  MOF  nanosheets  (MOFNs)  that featured  a  similar  planar  structure  were  put  forward  allowing  a more complete activation with efficient charge-transfer transitions induced  by  incident  photons  (Fig. 1(d))  [35].  Because  the accessible  coordination  sites  at  the  exposed  cluster  nodes  fully utilized the ligand-cluster unit instead of forming the 3D structure. A  typical  example  is  that  a  group  of  isostructural  tetrakis  (4- carboxyphenyl)  porphyrin  (TCPP)  based  MOF  compounds containing different metal ions was used as precursors to generate MOFNs by ultrasound-assisted exfoliation.
## 2.2    Single core–shell MOF substrates
Single  core–shell  MOF  substrates  are  normally  constructed  by SERS-active  plasmonic  nanoparticles  wrapped  in  MOF  shells because plasmonic nanoparticles can mainly contribute electromagnetic  enhancement  to  amplify  the  SERS  signals,  and MOFs  can  enrich  analytes  and  provide  certain  chemical enhancement  for  additional  SERS  enhancement.  By  the regulations of different metal ions and organic linkers, the micropore size of MOFs should conform to the analyte molecular size to selectively adsorb the analytes and the active sites should attract or bind the analytes to facilitate the charge transfer. As a result, the SERS  signals  can  be  remarkably  enhanced  for  those  specific analytes  that  are  selectively  adsorbed  and  effectively  enriched  at the  interface  of  MOF  and  plasmonic  nanoparticles.  Moreover, tailor-made core–shell MOFs improve the stability and service life of  the  substrate  through  resisting  oxidation  and  aggregation  for some traditional SERS-active plasmonic nanoparticles. To obtain the expected core–shell MOF, step-by-step growth and facile onestep synthesis approaches were the frequently reported routes.
Through  a  step-by-step  growth  method,  Hu  et  al.  reported  a shell  thickness  controllable  approach  by  a  versatile  step-by-step growth  to  build  Au@MIL-100(Fe)  (Fig. 2(a))  [36].  The  MIL100(Fe)  shell  can  stabilize  the  Au  nanoparticles  and  serve  as  an adsorptive  domain  to  enrich  analytes  in  proximity  to  the  metal core surface. During the process, the thickness of the MOF shell could  be  easily  tailored  by  repetition  times.  A  similar  approach was also employed to construct gold nanourchins wrapped with MIL-100(Cu)  (GNU@MIL-100(Cu))  (Fig. 2(b))  [37]. The  results from GNU@MIL-100(Cu) with 2 nm shell thickness demonstrated superior SERS activity and the intensity of the SERS signal gradually decreased when increasing the shell thickness. It should be noted that polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) was employed in the  aforementioned  examples  to  improve  the  stability  of nanoparticles  and  to  enhance  the  affinity  for  heterogeneous growth of the MOF shell on the surface of nanoparticles. Beyond the  PVP  agent,  inositol  hexaphosphate  (IP6)  and  thiolated polyethylene  glycol  (PEG)  also  demonstrated  the  same function [38–40].
Besides,  Zhang  et  al. $\mathrm{\DeltaAu@{ZIF8}}$ , Fig. 2(c))  [27],  PastorizaSantos  et  al. $(\operatorname{Au}@\operatorname{Ag}@\operatorname{ZIF}-8)$ [41],  Li  et  al.  (GNRs-QDs@NU901)  [42],  and  Liu  et  al.  (different  gold  plasmonic  nanoparticles encapsulated in ZIF-8, Figs. 3(a)–3(c)) [43] proposed the method of  synthesizing  core–shell  MOF  via  employing  plasmonic nanoparticles as seed and subsequently adding precursor solution of MOF to growth shell. Consequently, the thickness of the shell can be precisely controlled by adjusting the amount and the ratio of metal ion solution and organic ligand solution [38–40].
Compared  with  the  step-by-step  growth  method,  one-step synthesis is more conveniently handled and accelerates the whole synthesis  process.  The  thickness  and  size  of  core–shell nanoparticles  can  be  simply  adjusted  by  changing  the concentration of the metal precursors in the reaction solution. For instance, Liu et al. prepared Au@MOF-5 composite nanoparticles via  directly  mixing  both  the  Au  and  MOF  precursors  in  the solution in which the more amount of Au precursors was added, and  the  larger  gold  nanoparticle  cores  and  the  thinner  MOF-5 shells were obtained (Figs. 3(d)–3(f)) [44, 45]. It was noticed that ethanol  was  vital  for  the  formation  of  the  core–shell  structure because its presence could change the coordination environment of metal ions so that MOF-5 grew preferentially around the Au NPs instead of homogeneously dispersed in the reaction solution. Li et al. attained Au/MOF-74 with a shell thickness of about $3\mathrm{nm}$ through a similar synthesis method and confirmed the different concentrations of $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ during synthesis impact the MOF shell thickness [46].
In  contrast  with  facile  one-step  synthesis,  the  seed  growth approach is more complex in experimental operations but more flexible in composite construction. Plasmonic nanoparticles with different  nanostructures  and  plasmonic  array  substrates  [23, 47–49]  can  be  wrapped  in  MOFs  to  contribute  more electromagnetic  enhancement  to  SERS  enhancement  via  this route, while more facile one-step synthesis normally only attains nanosphere served as the core.
## 2.3    SERS-active  substrates based on  plasmonic nanoparticles embedded or attached porous MOF
“Ship-in-a-bottle” and “bottle-around-ship” strategies  are  two kinds of representative synthetic approaches to fabricate the SERSactive substrates based on plasmonic nanoparticles embedded or attached porous MOF. Metal precursors were added to the presynthesized  MOFs  and  yielded  plasmonic  nanoparticles  by subsequent reduction or decomposition of the precursors during the “ship-in-a-bottle” approach,  while  MOF  precursors  were directly  added  to  the  pre-synthesized  metal  nanoparticles  in  the “bottle-around-ship” approach [50]. In these strategies, the unique 3D  frameworks  with  abundant  micropores  allow  MOFs  to provide  enough  space  to  accommodate  more  available  SERSactive NPs, which not only contributes to the formation of more hot spots but also limits the distance among the SERS-active NPs to  prevent  their  over  migration  and  aggregation  [51].  Due  to electrostatic interactions and the intrinsic coordination property,
MOFs facilitate the enrichment of analyte molecules close to the inner SERS-active NPs, which further enhances the SERS signals.
In  the “ship-in-a-bottle” approach,  three-dimensional (3D) structures  of  MOFs  provide  ultrahigh  surface  area  to  embed plenty of plasmonic nanoparticles. The relatively simple synthetic process  makes  the “ship-in-a-bottle” approach  broadly  accepted although the formed morphology of the plasmonic nanoparticles in MOF micro-pores is hard to control and even the micro-pores of MOFs suffer the potential problem of collapse. Figure 4(a) gives a successful example, where AuNPs@MIL-101 was prepared via the  solution  impregnation  strategy  and  employed  as  the fluorescent/SERS dual-mode sensor. The sensor demonstrated the low detection limit (LOD) of part per trillion (ppt) level [52]. In the synthesis process, pre-synthesized MIL-101 was suspended in $\mathrm{HAuCl_{4}}$ aqueous solution and then reduced by sodium citrate to form gold nanoparticles in the micro-pores of MIL-101 (Figs. 4(b) and 4(c)). A similar step was also reported in the works of Hu et al. $\mathrm{\cdotAuNPs/MIL}{\cdot}101\mathrm{(Cr)}.$ , Fig. 4(d)) [56], Zhou et al. (AuNPs@MIL-101) [54], Sun et al. (UiO $\partial{\cdot}66(\mathrm{NH}_{2})@\mathrm{Au})$ [55], and Huang et al. (AgNPs/MIL-101(Fe), Fig. 4(e)) [53]. The density and size of embedded plasmonic nanoparticles directly depended on the  amount  of  metal  precursor  and  reducing  agent.  These promising  results  indicated  that  the  decorated  MOF  not  only exhibited  excellent  enrichment  but  also  endowed  plasmonic nanoparticles with more SERS-active sites. In addition, ultraviolet illumination  can  be  reductive  that  introduced  fewer  process organic agents and SERS background interference. Morphology of plasmonic  nanoparticles  could  be  adjusted  by  the  time  of ultraviolet illumination and the concentration of metal precursors. Gong and his coworkers synthesized a layer of dense $\mathrm{AgNPs/MIL}$ - $101(\mathrm{Cr})$ film by partial reduction of $\mathrm{Ag}$ ions on the surface of the film upon ultraviolet illumination, and the SERS performance of this substrate increased first and then decreased with the increase of the time of ultraviolet illumination [57]. Notably, the uniformity was proved by only about $5\%$ relative standard deviation (RSD) values of the major peak in detection.
Moreover,  the “bottle-around-ship” approach  can  well-define the  morphology  characteristics  of  plasmonic  nanoparticles  in
MOF,  whereas  the  growth  of  MOF  would  be  partly  restrained. Ag@Ni-MOF-1 was prepared by Zhang et al. by mixing trimesic acid,  nickel  chloride,  and  hexadecyl  trimethyl  ammonium bromide (CTAB) with Ag NPs to form a stacked structure with a hexagonal prism shape shown in Fig. 4(f) [58]. Moreover, in the experiment  of  4-mercaptobenzoic  acid  (MBA)  and  thiolfunctionalized  polystyrene  (PS-SH),  large-molecule  PS-SH  failed to  diffuse  into  the  micro-pores  of  Ag@Ni-MOF-1  and  interact with  the  embedded  Ag  NPs,  which  verified  the  Ag  NPs  tightly embedded in Ag@Ni-MOF-1 shown in Figs. $4(\mathrm{g})\mathrm{-4(i)}$ . Similarly, Sada et al. fabricated composite crystals of MOFs embedding Au nanorods  (Au  NR-MOFs)  by  the  direct  growth  of $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{bpdc})_{3}$ (bpdc $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 4{,}4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxilate)  on  the  surface  of  11- mercaptoundecanoic  acid  (MUA)  self-assembled  monolayercoated Au NRs for in situ monitoring diffusion of guest molecules from a piece of MOF crystals by SERS [59]. The SERS signals of guest molecules expressed a time-dependent phenomenon in the exchange  process  of  the  guest  molecules  from $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ to  N,Ndiethylformamide (DEF) and demonstrated that incorporation of Au NRs into MOF crystals did not lead to further resistance for guest molecules to transfer in micro-pores and come close to Au NR,  which  enabled  us  to  detect  guest  molecules  efficiently  by SERS.
## 2.4    Other synthetic strategies
With  the  development  of  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates, other novel and attractive synthesis methods were also reported. Their  design  strategies  and  SERS  enhancement  mechanisms  are varied. For instance, MOF derivatives and chiral structure in the fabrication  of  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  have  been recently investigated and their unique advantages also have been verified.  MOF  derivative  substrates  normally  utilize  MOFs  as  a template  to  load  tightly  ordered  and  well-dispersed  SERS-active NPs. In the design process of chiral structure, MOFs are modified with chiral ligands to provide enantioselective adsorption of chiral analytes and their chemical recognition.
One famous example is that Jin et al. reported that a derivative of silver MOF (Ag@Carbon) obtained from $\mathrm{Ag-MOF}$ undergoes thermal treatment to enable the uniformly distributed $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ to be insitu reduced  [60].  The  forming  plasmonic  Ag  NPs  were  welldispersed on the surface of carbonized MOF framework shown in Fig. 5(a). Due to the spatial distance of $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles directly depending  on  the  diameter  of  Ag-MOF  precursor  molecular  2- aminoterephthalic acid, the spacing distance between each $\operatorname{Ag}\operatorname{NP}$ is expected to be fixed so that the MOF-engineered Ag@Carbon exhibits  excellent  SERS  activity.  Moreover,  Dang  et  al.  prepared zinc oxide NPs derived from ZIF-8 by calcination to fabricate 3D $\mathrm{ZnO/Ag}$ substrate  by  evaporation-induced  self-assembly  of $\mathrm{Ag}$ NPs (Fig. 5(b)) [61]. The attained hierarchical porous structure of $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{nO}$ imparts  a  large  specific  surface  area  and  multi-active adsorption sites contributing to the formation of more hot spots and the enhancement of the sensitivity. Also, due to the excellent photocatalytic performance of ZnO, $\mathrm{\ZnO/Ag}$ substrate possesses excellent  reusability  because  various  analyte  molecules  can  be degraded under UV illumination.
The  utilization  of  chiral  ligands  in  the  synthesis  process  can attain homochiral MOFs (HMOFs) that provide an opportunity for  enantioselective  adsorption  of  chiral  analytes  and  their chemical recognition [64]. Both Guselnikova et al. (Fig. 5(c)) [62] and  O.  Lyutakov  et  al.  (Fig. 5(d))  [63]  functionalized  gold substrate  with  4-carboxybeneznediazonium  tosylate  (ADTCOOH)  served  as  linkers  for  subsequent  surface-assisted nucleation and growth of HMOF and chose optical enantiomers of chiral carboxylic acids served as chiral ligands for HMOF. The synergistic  performance  of  the  HMOF  substrates  resulted  in higher  enantioselectivity  than  existing  analogs  of  plasmonic materials.
## 3    Application in ultra-sensitive biomedical trace detection
Owing  to  a  variety  of  microstructure  and  unique  properties, MOFs demonstrate their merits in terms of enhancing the SERS effect through molecular enrichment and/or selective absorption. Hence,  tailor-made  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  would offer unexpected advantages and excellent sensing performance in detecting  trace  amounts  of  the  biomedical  marker,  even  under complex  background  interference,  such  as  single  or  multiple biomarkers,  metabolites,  and  pharmaceutical  ingredients  in human body exhales, fluids, and tissues [65].
## 3.1    Human exhalation
Human breath sample contains various VOCs which have been recognized as significant endogenous biomarker molecules closely associated  with  many  pathophysiological  processes.  Precisely tracking  these  important  VOCs  could  be  helpful  to  screening diverse diseases [66]. In order to precisely track the variation of these VOCs derived from human breath, high-performance VOC sensors  should  be  particularly  developed.  However,  most  of  the reported traditional breath sensors are easily affected by humidity in  human  exhalation  [56, 57].  In  contrast,  SERS  sensors  are normally water vapor insensitive. Besides, MOFs-based substrates have a large surface area and uniform micro-pores, which can be applied  as  solid-phase  microextraction  materials  for  rapid enrichment  and  selective  VOCs  molecular  adsorption.  These advantages provide new possibilities for improving SERS sensing performance  in  breath  analysis  [67].  A  ZIF-8  layer  coated  gold superparticle  (GSPs) $(\mathrm{GSPs}@Z\mathrm{IF}{-}8)$ )  substrate  was  reported  by Wang et al., which was capable of slowing the flow rate of gaseous biomarkers and capturing the VOCs molecules through a Schiff base reaction with 4-amino thiophenol grafted onto gold GSPs so that they can selectively detect biomarkers of lung malignancies at the  part  per  billion  (ppb)  level  (Fig. 6(a))  [22].  Another  typical example is that Li et al. designed a vapor generation paper-based thin-film  micro-extraction  platform  for  fluorescent  and  SERS sensing  of  volatile  benzaldehyde  (BA)  based  on  core–shell  gold nanorod-quantum  dots  (GNRs-QDs) $@$ NU-901  structures  to discriminate lung cancer patients (Fig. 6(b)) [42].
## 3.2    Human body fluid
By utilizing MOF-based SERS-active substrates, biomarkers with low concentrations and complex background interference in body fluids such as blood [62, 68–72], urine [56, 73], and saliva [74] in vitro could  be  enriched  and  distinguished  to  help  the  screening and diagnosis of many diseases.
Figure  7(a) depicts  an  integrated  SERS  platform  with  analyte enrichment and analyte filtration function (referred to as the ASERS  platform)  to  detect  4-nitrobenzenethiol  (4-NBT)  at nanomolar-level concentrations in whole blood [70]. The analytes were  effectively  enriched  into  the  SERS  sensors  after  solvent evaporation  by  incorporating  the  slippery  polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) brush surface capable of shrinking the analyte dispersion area and adjusting the aperture size of the MOF shell. Choo et al. developed novel SERS-active MOF–gold nanoparticle complexes by  attaching  citrate-capped  AuNPs  to  the  surface  of  aminefunctionalized  MOFs  (Fig. 7(b))  [71].  When  MOF–AuNP complexes  were  added  to  human  serum  for  diagnosing  iron deficiency, transferrin (Tf) in the human serum acted as an anchor to form a three-dimensional assembly of MOF–AuNP units via specific binding to the Fe(III) ions of adjacent MOF units. Besides, a  peroxidase-mimicking  nano-enzyme  consisting  of  silver nanoparticles  was  placed  on  a  MOF  surface  to  monitor  blood cholesterol. This biosensor provided a dynamic detection range of $1.0\mathrm{-}100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and a relatively low limit of detection near $0.36~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [75].  A  cerium  MOF-loaded  silver  nanocluster  was  created. It  can  detect  dopamine  in  human  serum  by  dual  SERS  and resonance  Rayleigh  scattering  spectroscopy  with  a  detection concentration range of $0.01{-}0.25~\mathrm{nmol/L}$ and a detection limit of
0.008 nmol/L [76].
For  the  analysis  of  urine,  a  capillary  driven  microfluidic  chip based  immunoassay  was  developed  via  the  antibody-modified magnetic MOFs as a capture probe. This microfluidic chip could detect human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) protein prohibited by  the  World  Anti-Doping  Agency  (WADA),  whose  regression coefficient  and  limit  of  detection  were  found  as  0.9985  and $0.61\ \mathrm{IU}/\mathrm{L},$ respectively [73]. Moreover, based on the peroxidaselike  activity  of  Fe-MOFs,  Huang  et  al.  developed $\mathrm{AgNPs/MIL}.$ 101(Fe)  by in  situ synthesis  of  Ag  NPs  on  the  surface  of  MIL101(Fe)  to  detect  dopamine  by  utilizing  enzyme-linked immunosorbent  assay  (ELISA)  colorimetric  substrate  as  a  SERS marker, as shown in Fig. 7(c) [53]. This new method showed good linearity in the range from $1.054\mathrm{\pM}$ to $210.8~\mathrm{nM}$ for dopamine with the detection limit of approximately $0.32\mathrm{pM}$ .
By in situ modification of Au NPs onto two-dimensional (2D) metalloporphyrins  MOF  (Cu-tetra(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin chloride(Fe(III)),  Wu  et  al.  synthesized  AuNPs/Cu-TCPP(Fe)  to induce the cascade catalysis reactions and convert glucose in saliva into hydrogen peroxide $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ which can oxidize the no Ramanactive  leucomalachite  green  (LMG)  into  the  Raman-active malachite green (MG) (Fig. 7(d)) [74]. The quantitative detection of  glucose  without  the  disturbance  from  fructose,  lactose,  and maltose in saliva was determined to be a linear range from 0.16 to $8\ \mathrm{mmol/L}$ $\left(R^{2}=0.993\right)$ with  a  limit  of  detection  of $3.9\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/L},$ which has great potential to be convenient for diabetic patients to monitor their glucose noninvasively.
## 3.3    Bio-imaging
Due  to  the  prominent  advantages  of  ultra  sensitivity,  noninvasiveness,  biocompatibility,  and  anti-photobleaching,  Au  NPbased  SERS  imaging  has  been  recognized  as  an  ideal  diagnostic and theranostic system [77, 78]. Meanwhile, with adjustable pore size  and  incredibly  ultrahigh  internal  surface  areas  and hydrophilic/hydrophobic  cavities  to  hold  diverse  drugs  [79], MOFs  are  potential  nanomaterials  to  fabricate  multifunctional theranostic  platforms  [80].  Applicable  assembling  of  SERS materials  with  MOFs  can  integrate  chemical  and  biomedical properties for constructing a novel platform of versatile imaging and therapy.
As shown in Fig. 8(a), a functional platform was developed for the  imaging  of  tumor  tissue  of  human  colon  cancer  with  SERS imaging  of  HeLa,  MCF-7,  LNCaP,  QGY-7703,  HCT116,  and MDA-MB-231  cells  and  drug  carrier  of  the  anti-cancer  drug doxorubicin  (DOX)  based  on  ZIF-8-coated  Au@Ag  core–shell nanorod  (Au@AgNR@ZIF-8)  [81].  A  strong  Raman  signal  was generated by a reporter molecule of 4-aminothiophenol (4-ATP). In  addition,  Horcajada  et  al. constructed  an  Au@ZIF-8 nanocomposite for the process monitoring and thermoplasmonicdriven release of encapsulated active molecules inside living cells (Fig. 8(b)) [82]. The wrapped amphiphilic polymers can prevent ZIF-8 degradation and drug leakage into aqueous media or inside living cells, with SERS imaging and photothermal effects.
## 4    Summary and outlook
In  this  perspective,  we  have  given  an  overview  of  the  synthesis strategies  of  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  and  their emerging applications in the biomedical field. Three mainstream strategies  developed  to  fabricate  MOF-based  SERS-active substrates  have  been  summarized  in  detail,  including  (1)  singlecomponent  MOF  substrates,  (2)  core–shell  MOF  substrates,  (3)
SERS-active  substrates  based  on  plasmonic  nanoparticles embedded  or  attached  porous  MOF,  and  (4)  other  synthetic strategies.  Normally,  the  SERS  enhancement  of  electromagnetic enhancement  is  significantly  higher  than  that  of  chemical enhancement. Compared to other synthesis strategies, core–shell MOF  substrates  are  the  most  common  and  effective,  because plasmonic  nanoparticles  and  plasmonic  arrays  with  different nanostructures can be wrapped in MOFs to synergistically boost the  SERS  enhancement.  Plasmonic  nanoparticles  and  plasmonic arrays  can  mainly  contribute  electromagnetic  enhancement  to amplify the SERS signals, and the MOF shells can enrich analytes and  provide  certain  chemical  enhancement  for  additional  SERS enhancement.
With high tailorability and unique properties, suitably designed MOFs  show  prominent  potential  to  assist  traditional  SERS platforms to integrate electromagnetic and chemical enhancement.  For  example,  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates can  absorb/enrich  target  molecules  and  be  not  disturbed  by humidity  so  that  the  platform  can  easily  deal  with  the  complex background  interference  from  human  body  exhales,  fluids,  and tissues.  In  order  to  further  improve  the  SERS  enhancement  of MOF-based SERS-active substrates, researchers should pay more attention to (1) matching the pore size of MOFs with the analyte molecules (e.g., acid etching and selecting different organic links); (2) adjusting the energy band and HOMO–LOMO levels between MOFs  and  analyte  molecules  to  improve  charge  transfer;  (3) creating  more  active  sites  on  the  surface  of  MOFs  to  attract  or bind  more  analyte  molecules;  (4)  avoiding  the  overlapping characteristic  peaks  between  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates and  analyte  molecules;  and  (5)  carrying  out  hydrophilic  and hydrophobic  surface  modification  and  morphology  to  adapt  to different states of biomolecules.
Despite  a  novel  trend  in  the  development  of  SERS,  the combination  of  MOFs  with  the  SERS  technique  has  exhibited excellent performance and advantages in biomedical applications. MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  still  have  their  inherent limitations and challenges for biomedical trace detection that need to be considered and solved. (1) MOFs made up of organic ligands and  other  organic  solvents  used  in  synthesis  progress  like  the capping  agents  may  have  several  its  own  Raman  characteristic peaks the same as that of the biomarkers to be analyzed. (2) The enhancement  mechanism  of  MOFs  in  SERS  has  many  unclear contents  to  be  explored.  HOMO–LOMO  level  matching  by density functional theory is also complicated. Thus, the simulation calculation  of  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  for  specific biomedical  detection  is  relatively  difficult  to  carry  out.  (3)  The reproducibility in the actual detection of substrates depends on the spatial  arrangements  of  plasmonic  nanoparticles.  It’s  also  a challenge to keep the uniform morphology features in large areas for  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates.  (4)  Some  solvents  and reagents in the MOF synthesis process may be toxic or have poor biocompatibility that limits their application scenarios.
Despite the existing challenges, we can certainly anticipate that next-generation  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  will  have  a bright future. With the rapid development of related supporting equipment  and  artificial  intelligence  algorithm,  the  special customized  design  of  MOF-based  SERS-active  substrates  will  be broadly mature and exhibit excellent ultra-sensitive performance with multifunction in biomedical investigations.
